{
  "dom_id": "step-q115856",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14093672/assets/bba667daa6bfd40eeb5450cc86066184819b903ace2641bc58c6b5c83869a4b6.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{\u2032}={y}^{2}-2y$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}={y}^{2}-4y+4$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=2y-{y}^{2}$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14093672/assets/72801ec62e5e23173097ef674169b26c85fab65cbf543af44b3582f0e14fd0c4.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\u2197$ | $\u2198$ | $\u2197$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}={y}^{2}-2y=y(y-2)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}={y}^{2}-4y+4=(y-2)^{2}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=2$ only.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=2y-{y}^{2}=y(2-y)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\u2198$ | $\u2197$ | $\u2198$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
